    "prologue": "<p class=\"w-lead\">Thirteen parts have been about the land between the rivers, and since 1947 almost entirely about the eastern half of it. This part fixes that, and then follows Punjabis out of the subcontinent altogether.</p><p>Part 13 covered Indian Punjab from the publication of the Radcliffe line to the present day. It began on 17 August 1947, when Punjabis were finally told which country they were in, two days after independence.</p><p>It went through the killing, which was organised rather than mad, and which nobody counted — estimates still range from 200,000 to two million. It went through the walking: roughly ten million people crossing in both directions in about four months, almost none of it planned, because every leadership had publicly assumed minorities would stay.</p><p>Then it followed what east Punjab built out of half a province. The standard acre and the graded cut, which resettled millions of farming families and left the landless with nothing to claim. A capital built from scratch at Chandigarh, which both halves then argued over. Nineteen years of refusal over the Punjabi Suba, settled in 1966 by a division that left the capital and the river water unresolved. The Green Revolution, which fed India and spent an aquifer. The 1980s, which are still being argued about and which no court or commission has closed. And a present of falling income rank, debt, emigration, and a water table dropping under the fields.</p><p>What Part 13 did not do is look west. That is the gap this part fills, and it is a large one.</p><div class=\"w-box w-box--number\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">In Real Terms</span><p>Indian Punjab has roughly 30 million people. Pakistani Punjab has roughly 128 million.</p><p>So for every Punjabi in India there are around four in Pakistan. When people say \"Punjab\" and mean the Indian state, they are naming about a fifth of the Punjabis in the world.</p><p>Add several million more in Britain, Canada, the United States, Australia and the Gulf, and the picture changes again. This series has spent thirteen parts on a homeland and one part is now owed to everybody who is not in the smaller half of it.</p></div><h3 class=\"w-h3\">How this book is built#</h3><p>Six kinds of box run through every part of this series. If you have arrived at the last part first, here is what each one does.</p><div class=\"w-box w-box--simple\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">Word Box</span><p><strong>What it does:</strong> explains a hard word the moment it first appears, so you never have to carry an unexplained term or go hunting in a glossary.</p></div><div class=\"w-box w-box--number\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">In Real Terms</span><p><strong>What it does:</strong> turns a number you cannot picture into something you can. You have just seen one.</p></div><div class=\"w-box w-box--answer\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">How We Actually Know This</span><p><strong>What it does:</strong> shows the evidence itself — the census column, the recruitment register, the remittance figure — and says plainly what it can and cannot prove.</p></div><div class=\"w-box w-box--arg\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">The Argument</span><p><strong>What it does:</strong> sets out a question where serious people disagree. Each side gets its strongest honest case, then a verdict block says where things stand and what would settle it.</p><div class=\"w-verdict\"><strong>The promise, one last time:</strong> I am not going to pick a side for you. This part contains arguments that are live in three countries at once, and in one case between two governments. The promise holds anyway.</div></div><div class=\"w-box w-box--weigh\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">The Hidden Assumption</span><p><strong>What it does:</strong> digs out something a claim quietly takes for granted without proving. It is the signature of this series, and this part turns it on the series itself before the end.</p></div><div class=\"w-box w-box--remember\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">Remember This</span><p><strong>What it does:</strong> closes every chapter with the key points in the plainest words available. Read only these and you would still have the story.</p></div><h3 class=\"w-h3\">A warning about this part#</h3><p>Part 13 warned that its material was inside living memory. This part has a different problem: much of it is inside living <em>politics</em>.</p><p>Some of what follows is contested between Pakistan's provinces. Some is contested between India and Canada, at the level of expelled diplomats. Some concerns organisations that are legal in one country and banned in another. Writing about any of it invites the reader to check which passport the author holds.</p><p>I hold an Indian one. You should know that, and you should read this part with it in mind, particularly Chapters Three, Nine and the Afterword. Where I think my own position is likely to be pulling at me, I have said so in the text rather than hoping you would not notice.</p>",
